[38;5;231m⏺[39m I'll sketch the options in a browser tab so they're easier to compare. It should open on its own; if it doesn't, use
  this URL:

  [94m]8;id=8pd9u6;http://localhost:51757/?key=c668b9052a7ea16602418fc6814885ee955a65115e0589915f56080729b6b7db\http://localhost:51757/?key=c668b9052a7ea16602418fc6814885ee955a65115e0589915f56080729b6b7db[39m]8;;\

  (The browser view uses a lot of tokens, so tell me if you'd rather stay in the terminal.)

  [1mWhat's on screen:[0m three mockups of the settings page. Each sorts the 12 fields in [38;5;153mpublic/settings.html[39m into the same
  four groups:

  - [1mProfile:[0m display name, email, avatar URL
  - [1mPreferences:[0m time zone, language, theme
  - [1mNotifications:[0m email, push, weekly digest
  - [1mSecurity:[0m two-factor auth, session timeout, API token

  The three layouts:

  - [1mA. Headed sections:[0m one column, with a heading and divider between groups. This is the smallest change.
  - [1mB. Separate cards:[0m each group sits in its own panel, which separates them more clearly. Still a single page.
  - [1mC. Sidebar nav:[0m a menu on the left shows one group at a time. This handles growth best, but it needs new JavaScript,
    and fields in other groups are hidden from view.

  I recommend [1mA or B[0m. Both are plain HTML and CSS. The form keeps the same field names, so [38;5;153msettings.js[39m and the data it
  sends don't change. C is only worth it if you expect many more settings soon.

  Take a look and tell me here which one you prefer. You can also click an option in the browser to select it. Are the
  four groups right, too?

  Note: the companion saves its mockups in [38;5;153m.hyperpowers/[39m in this repo. There's no [38;5;153m.gitignore[39m yet, so that folder should
  be added to one.

